<h2>TWIA, flood, and CRS for Port Aransas</h2>
<p>Port Aransas is in Nueces County, which is in the TWIA designated catastrophe area. Many owners carry a homeowners or condo policy that excludes wind and hail, a TWIA windstorm policy, and a flood policy. TWIA claims generally must be filed within one year of the date of loss; see our <a href="/services/twia-windstorm-claims/">TWIA windstorm claims</a> page and the article on the <a href="/twia-claim-process-corpus-christi/">TWIA claim process</a>.</p>
<p>Flood coverage needs particular attention on the island. Properties in V, VE, or V1-30 zones that were built, altered, remodeled, or enlarged on or after September 1, 2009 must show proof of NFIP coverage, when available, to get TWIA coverage. Based on FEMA Community Status Book data (April 2025), Port Aransas participates in the NFIP but not the Community Rating System. NFIP residential limits are $250,000 for the building and $100,000 for contents, and coverage below the lowest elevated floor is often limited, so read your flood policy carefully. Our <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> and <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge</a> pages explain the split.</p>
<p>Roof repairs and re-roofs in the catastrophe area fall under the TDI Windstorm Inspection Program, and TWIA eligibility depends on a windstorm certificate of compliance for work done on or after January 1, 1988. For City of Port Aransas permit rules, contact the city's building department before repairs begin.</p>

<h2>How we help on Mustang Island</h2>
<p>We inspect roofs, building envelopes, balconies, windows and doors, mechanical equipment, and interiors, and we photograph and measure everything for an itemized estimate. For rental owners, we also look at whether the policy includes loss of rents or business income coverage. For condo buildings, we review both the master policy and unit-owner policies. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> and <a href="/services/hurricane-damage-claims/">hurricane damage claims</a> pages cover more.</p>
<p>Because many Port Aransas properties are second homes or short-term rentals, owners are often away when a storm hits. Report the claim as soon as you know about damage, ask a trusted neighbor or property manager to photograph the property before cleanup, and keep records of any emergency repairs. Delays in reporting can complicate a claim, and TWIA's one-year filing deadline still applies. As a Corpus Christi Public Adjuster serving Mustang Island, we can inspect the property and coordinate with you remotely if you are out of town.</p>
<p>Live tide readings for Port Aransas are on our <a href="/weather/">Corpus Christi weather and tides page</a>. When you are ready, request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call or text [[phone]].</p>
